---
import Callout from "../../components/Callout.astro";
import PageShell from "../../components/PageShell.astro";
import MainLayout from "../../layouts/MainLayout.astro";
---

<MainLayout title="Documentation - AI Training Guide">
	<PageShell chapterId="development" subChapterId="documentation">
		<div class="prose space-y-6">
			<p>
				Documentation isn't just for humans anymore. Every piece of documentation you write
				serves double duty: it helps the next developer who touches the code, and it helps
				the AI understand your project when it's asked to make changes. Good documentation
				has always been valuable. With AI tools, it's become one of our most important activities.
			</p>

			<p>
				Earlier in the guide we recommended{" "}
				<a href="/requirements-gathering/documentation-first-development" class="text-teal-600 hover:text-teal-800">documentation-first development</a>: write the docs before the code, and let the docs in your <code>docs/</code> folder be the source of truth that both people and AI build from. This page is the other half of that habit. Documentation-first is how the docs get created; what follows is how you keep them alive while you write code, so they stay accurate enough to be worth reading on the next task.
			</p>

			<h3>Why Documentation Matters More Now</h3>

			<p>
				When you ask an AI to modify a codebase, it reads whatever context it can find.
				If your code has clear comments explaining <em>why</em> something works a certain way,
				the AI is far less likely to break it. If your architecture decisions are documented
				somewhere the AI can reach, it produces code that fits the existing patterns instead
				of inventing its own.
			</p>

			<p>
				Think about what happens without documentation. A developer asks the AI to add a new
				feature. The AI scans the code, makes assumptions about why things are structured the
				way they are, and writes something that technically works but doesn't match the team's
				approach. The developer spends 30 minutes in code review catching issues that a single
				paragraph of context would have prevented.
			</p>

			<Callout variant="success" title="Multiplying Effect">
				<p>
					Documentation you write once gets used by the AI in every conversation. A 10-minute
					investment in writing a clear approach document saves time across dozens of AI
					interactions, for every developer on the team.
				</p>
			</Callout>

			<h3>What to Document</h3>

			<p>
				Not all documentation is equally useful for AI-assisted development. Focus on the
				types that have the biggest impact on AI output quality.
			</p>

			<div class="space-y-4 my-8">
				<div class="border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4">
					<div class="text-sm font-semibold text-stone-800 mb-1">
						Architecture decisions
					</div>
					<p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">
						Why did you choose this database? Why is authentication handled in middleware
						instead of per-route? Why does the billing module have its own API client?
						These "why" documents prevent the AI from making changes that look reasonable
						in isolation but conflict with deliberate design choices. These are Architecture
						Decision Records, and in a documentation-first repo they live in{" "}
						<code>docs/decisions/</code>. They are some of the highest-value context you can
						give an AI, because they encode the constraints it must not violate.
					</p>
				</div>

				<div class="border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4">
					<div class="text-sm font-semibold text-stone-800 mb-1">
						Approach documents
					</div>
					<p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">
						Short documents that describe how a particular feature or system works at a high
						level. "The notification system uses a pub/sub pattern. Events are published to
						a central bus and individual handlers subscribe to specific event types." This
						gives the AI enough context to add a new notification type without reading
						every file in the system. In a documentation-first repo these are your feature
						specs and tech notes, living in <code>docs/features/</code> and <code>docs/tech/</code>.
					</p>
				</div>

				<div class="border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4">
					<div class="text-sm font-semibold text-stone-800 mb-1">
						Code comments on non-obvious logic
					</div>
					<p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">
						You don't need to comment every line. But when something looks wrong but is
						actually intentional, a comment saves the AI (and future developers) from
						"fixing" it. <code>// Intentionally using setTimeout(0) to defer to next tick
						for DOM measurement</code> prevents a well-meaning cleanup.
					</p>
				</div>

				<div class="border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4">
					<div class="text-sm font-semibold text-stone-800 mb-1">
						API contracts and data shapes
					</div>
					<p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">
						TypeScript types help a lot here, but a brief description of what each API
						endpoint expects and returns, especially edge cases, helps the AI write
						code that handles real-world scenarios instead of just the happy path.
					</p>
				</div>
			</div>

			<h3>What Not to Over-Document</h3>

			<p>
				There's a balance. Documentation that restates what the code already says clearly
				is noise, and noise in the context window pushes out useful information.
			</p>

			<Callout variant="warning" title="Skip these">
				<ul class="space-y-1 list-none p-0 m-0">
					<li>
						<span class="font-bold">-</span> Comments that restate the code:{" "}
						<code>// increment counter</code> above <code>counter++</code>
					</li>
					<li>
						<span class="font-bold">-</span> Auto-generated JSDoc that just repeats
						parameter names without adding meaning
					</li>
					<li>
						<span class="font-bold">-</span> Changelog-style comments inside files - leave this in Git
					</li>
					<li>
						<span class="font-bold">-</span> Documentation of every possible
						configuration option when sensible defaults exist
					</li>
				</ul>
			</Callout>

			<h3>When a Skill Is the Better Home</h3>

			<p>
				Not every recurring piece of knowledge belongs in a static document. A doc
				describes how something works; a{" "}
				<a href="/requirements-writing/skills" class="text-teal-600 hover:text-teal-800">skill</a>{" "}
				tells the AI how to carry out a task the same way every time. When you catch
				yourself pasting the same instructions into prompt after prompt ("here's how we
				write a migration," "here's the shape of a new API route"), that repetition is the
				signal to promote it from a doc into a skill.
			</p>

			<p>
				The difference that matters is loading. A document sits in <code>docs/</code> until
				someone points the AI at it, while a skill's description tells the AI when to pull
				it in on its own, so the procedure shows up exactly when the matching task comes up
				and stays out of the context window the rest of the time. Reach for a skill when the
				knowledge is a repeatable procedure; keep it in a doc when it's reference material or
				the reasoning behind a decision.
			</p>

			<h3>Using AI to Write Documentation</h3>

			<p>
				AI is excellent at writing documentation for existing code.
				You can point it at a module and ask it to explain the architecture, describe the
				data flow, or summarize the public API. The AI reads code much faster than you do,
				and it can produce a solid first draft of documentation in seconds.
			</p>

			<p>
				Key word: <strong>first draft</strong>. AI-written documentation needs review
				just like AI-written code. The AI might miss the "why" behind decisions (it can see
				what the code does, not why you chose that approach). It might describe behavior
				that's actually a bug rather than intentional. And it tends to be more verbose than
				necessary.
			</p>

			<Callout variant="example" title="Good prompt for documentation">
				<p class="font-mono">
					"Based on what was written in this ticket, and the work done in this PR, update the API documentation at docs/api.md. Make sure to include the why behind the decisions and keep it short and sweet."
				</p>
			</Callout>

			<p>
				Notice what the prompt does: it points the AI at concrete sources (the ticket and
				the PR) so the draft is grounded in what actually changed, asks it to capture the
				why behind the decisions rather than just restating behavior, and tells it to keep
				things short. You still review the result, but you start from an accurate, grounded
				draft instead of a blank page.
			</p>

			<h3>Documentation as a Feedback Loop</h3>

			<p>
				Here's a pattern that works well: after the AI completes a task, ask it to update
				the relevant documentation. "You just added the webhook handler. Update
				docs/notifications.md to include how webhooks fit into the notification system."
				The AI has all the context fresh, so the documentation it produces is accurate and
				specific.
			</p>

			<p>
				Over time, this creates a self-reinforcing cycle. Better documentation leads to
				better AI output, which leads to less time fixing AI mistakes, which leaves more
				time for documentation. The teams that invest in this loop early see compounding
				returns as the codebase grows.
			</p>

			<Callout variant="tip" title="Practical tip">
				<p>
					Make "update the docs" part of your definition of done for any task. It only takes a couple minutes to update documentation. That small investment pays dividends
					across every future AI interaction with that part of the codebase.
				</p>
			</Callout>
		</div>
	</PageShell>
</MainLayout>
